<section xml:id="sec_gauss2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Application: Infinite Line of Charge</title> 

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in a 50-minute classroom, after students have completed:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_symmetry"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_area-vectors"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_flux"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_gauss"/>
			</p>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		The last activity in <xref ref="sec_gauss"/> makes a good warm-up for the class.
	</p>
  </assemblage>

  <subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
	
  </subsubsection>



	<activity xml:id="class_gauss-infinite-line">
		<title>The Infinite Line of Charge</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				Suppose you have an infinitely long wire with a uniform charge density <m>\lambda</m>.  An imaginary cylindrical Gaussian surface with radius <m>s</m> and height <m>h</m> is centered on the wire.  Point P is on the right edge of the Gaussian cylinder.
			</p>
			<figure xml:id="fig_cylinder">
		<title>Gaussian Cylinder Figure</title>
    	<caption>An infinite wire and a cylindrical Gaussian surface.</caption>
    		<image source="cylinder.png" width = "75%" >
          <description>A long, straight vertical wire with charge density lambda and a coaxial Gaussian cylinder with height h, radius s, and point P at its right edge.</description>
        </image>
    </figure>
		</introduction>
		<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What direction do you expect the electric field to be at point P?  How does the direction change as you move around the cylinder?
					</p>
				</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Is the flux through the top endcap positive, negative, or zero?  What about the bottom endcap?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Is the flux through the outside surface of the cylinder positive, negative, or zero?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use Gauss's Law to solve for the electric field at point P.
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							Find the charge enclosed in the surface.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Calculate the flux through each of the three surfaces.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Use this to find the net flux.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Use Gauss's Law to solve for the magnitude of the electric field!
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Why would Gauss's Law be a poor choice if you wanted to solve for the electric field due to a wire with <em>finite</em> length <m>L</m>?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</activity>


<!--NO AI-->
</section>

